@*
    What kind of thing a chapter describes, drawn as a classification.

    `kind` is a closed vocabulary — `format`, `runtime`, `framework`, `library` —
    and that is the fact worth drawing. A bare `framework` in a code span is a value
    that could have come from any field in the block; an outlined chip says "this
    entry has been filed as one of a small number of things", which is what the
    field means and what lets the row lose its label.

    `.badge--kind` shares its rule with `.badge--area` because the two are the same
    fact about different things: the drawer something is filed in, out of a
    vocabulary somebody else owns. Outlined and unfilled, since a classification is
    not something the product acts on.

    No slug, and deliberately no per-value tone. The vocabulary belongs to the
    folder rather than to this library, so a colour per value would be this
    component asserting which kinds matter — and a `Slug` would put an authored word
    into a class name, which is how `.badge--kind-framework` becomes a rule nobody
    wrote.

    The value is drawn as authored. A stray capital in a `kind` is the author's, and
    the vocabulary is not checked here: a word the folder does not define is a typo
    worth seeing, not one worth silently normalising.
*@

@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Kind))
{
    <Badge Kind="kind" Title="@TitleValue" CssClass="@CssClass" TestId="@TestId">@Kind</Badge>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The kind, exactly as the file wrote it. Blank draws nothing — a
    /// field the file did not state, rather than a chip asserting emptiness.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Kind { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The tooltip. Defaulted to the field name and the value, because the
    /// row this is drawn in hides its label: a pointer user has nothing else on
    /// screen telling them which field the chip came from.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Title { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A modifier on top of the badge's own classes.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    private string TitleValue => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Title) ? $"kind: {Kind}" : Title;
}
